.TH MKCONNECT 1 "November 2026" "MK BBS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkconnect \- a terminal program for bulletin board systems, with a dialing directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkconnect
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B mkconnect
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]
.br
.B mkconnect
[\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-e\fR \fIname\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkconnect
calls bulletin board systems over the network, in the way of QModem on DOS: a list of systems (the dialing directory), a terminal that
shows the screens of the system as they were drawn, file transfers, login scripts, a log.
It runs in an ordinary text terminal and does not emulate one: the terminal in which it runs does the work, and
.B mkconnect
turns the IBM PC character set (CP437) of the system into UTF-8 where the terminal wants it and keeps the colours of the art as drawn.
The bottom row of the screen is a status bar with the name of the system, the time on line, the character set and the progress of a transfer.
.PP
Without a host or a name the dialing directory is shown.
The phone book,
.IR ~/.config/mkconnect/phonebook ,
is sealed with a start password that is asked when the program starts (ChaCha20, HMAC\-SHA256, PBKDF2); an empty password at the first start
leaves it as a plain text file (mode 600).
It can hold the login name and the password of each system.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t " " telnet | telnets | raw | ssh
the kind of connection (default telnet).
.B telnets
is Telnet inside TLS; the certificate of the system is not checked.
.B ssh
runs the system ssh on a pseudo terminal; \fIuser@host\fR may be given.
.B raw
is a plain TCP connection.
.TP
.B \-u
the terminal wants UTF-8: the CP437 of the system is converted.
.TP
.B \-c
the terminal does not: the bytes are passed as they are.
Without either, the type of the terminal (\fBTERM\fR) decides, as for mkcat(1).
.TP
.BI \-e " name"
call a system of the phone book by its name.
.TP
.BI \-b " file"
another phone book.
.TP
.BI \-s " script"
run a script after the connect: a file, one in
.IR ~/.config/mkconnect/scripts ,
or a built in one (synchronet, mk).
It wins over the script of the entry.
.TP
.BI \-d " folder"
where downloaded files come (default
.I ~/Downloads
when it is there, else the current folder).
.TP
.BI \-r " rounds" ", " \-w " seconds"
multi\-dial: how many times the marked systems are tried (3) and the wait between two tries (5).
.SH IN A CALL
.TP
.B Ctrl\-]
the attention key: a menu on the bottom row (a second Ctrl\-] sends one to the system).
.TP
.B Alt\-P
send the password of the system and a carriage return.
.B Alt\-N
sends the login name.
.TP
.B Alt\-U, Alt\-L
upload, download: Zmodem, Ymodem, Ymodem\-G, Xmodem or Xmodem\-1K.
A Zmodem sender that the system starts is taken up at once.
Esc or Ctrl\-X cancels.
.TP
.B Alt\-C
capture on or off (a file \fIname\-date.cap\fR in the current folder).
.B Alt\-T
changes the character set of the display.
.TP
.B Alt\-H, Alt\-D, Alt\-X
hang up; hang up and go to the dialer; hang up and leave.
.PP
An Alt key is the Esc key and a letter pressed together.
.SH THE DIALING DIRECTORY
Enter dials, A adds, E edits, D deletes, F or / finds, S sorts, Space marks, M is multi\-dial (the marked systems, or the selected one, are tried in
turn with a pause, round after round, until one answers; Esc stops), P changes the start password, F1 helps, Q or Alt\-X leaves.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/mkconnect/phonebook
the dialing directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mkconnect/scripts/
scripts (MKHubScr language: expect, send, when, upload, download, variables login, password, name, host, protocol).
.TP
.I ~/.config/mkconnect/calls.log
a line for each call, transfer and script.
.SH SEE ALSO
mkcat(1), mkdraw(1), ssh(1).
.PP
MKCONNECT.md in the documentation.
